Q1. GraphQL is an API query language where clients request exactly the data fields they need.

Q2. It solves over-fetching, under-fetching, and needing many REST endpoints or requests for related data.

Q3. The backend defines a schema; clients send queries to read data and mutations to change data, usually through one GraphQL endpoint.

Q4. REST uses multiple resource endpoints and fixed responses. GraphQL usually uses one endpoint and lets the client choose the response fields. REST is simpler for normal CRUD; GraphQL is useful when clients need different data shapes.
